.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt ZPOOL-PARITY 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zpool-parity
.Nd show or discard the parity records of a ZFS storage pool, or reclaim leaked space
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zpool
.Cm parity
.Cm status
.Op Fl Hp
.Op Ar pool
.Nm zpool
.Cm parity
.Cm drop
.Op Fl f
.Ar pool
.Nm zpool
.Cm parity
.Cm reclaim
.Ar pool
.Op Ar zdb-output
.Nm zpool
.Cm parity
.Cm reclaim
.Fl r
.Ar pool
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A parity set is a group of independent single-disk pools that share lagging
parity disks.
Each pool in the set stays a normal pool.
It imports and reads alone on any host that supports its features, without the
parity disks and without any parity software.
.Pp
A pool becomes a member of the set when its
.Sy parityset
property is
.Sy on .
The pool then keeps records of the blocks that it allocates and frees, and
it holds each freed block until the user-space daemon
.Nm zparityd
has applied the change to the parity disks.
The parity lags the writes: a write never waits for the parity, unless the pool
reaches a limit and
.Sy parity_onstall
is
.Sy wait .
The records use the
.Sy parity_records
feature.
See
.Xr zpoolprops 7
and
.Xr zpool-features 7 .
.Pp
Parity accepts a rebuilt block only if its bytes pass the checksum of the block.
A block that has no checksum cannot pass.
Therefore a member pool refuses
.Sy checksum Ns = Ns Sy off
on its datasets.
For a block that was written with
.Sy checksum Ns = Ns Sy off
before the pool joined the set, the parity software gets the bytes marked as not
verified, and the pool refuses to verify or to repair that block.
See
.Xr zpoolprops 7 .
.Pp
A replace of the disk of a member, and the start of a hot spare in its place,
is always a sequential rebuild, as with
.Nm zpool Cm replace Fl s ,
also when
.Fl s
is not given.
The parity software works on raw bytes, so the new disk must hold every range
that the space maps call allocated.
A healing resilver copies only the live blocks.
It misses the held freed blocks, and the blocks that wait to be freed after the
destroy of a snapshot or of a dataset.
A sequential rebuild copies all of them.
The pool then starts a scrub, which checks the live blocks.
The replace fails if the
.Sy device_rebuild
feature is not enabled.
.Pp
This parity is not the parity of a
.Sy raidz
vdev.
.Nm zpool Cm status
shows the parity of
.Sy raidz
vdevs only.
.Pp
To arm a pool that was rebuilt from parity before its first write, import it
with
.Nm zpool Cm import Fl o Sy parityset Ns = Ns Sy on .
A read-only import loads the records so that
.Nm zpool Cm parity Cm status
can show them, but it never writes.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Nm zpool
.Cm parity
.Cm status
.Op Fl Hp
.Op Ar pool
.Xc
Displays the parity state of
.Ar pool ,
or of all imported pools if no pool is given.
The output shows:
.Bl -tag -compact -offset 4n -width "applied_txg"
.It Sy state
.Sy off ,
.Sy on ,
.Sy draining
.Pq the property is Sy off No but records remain ,
or
.Sy dropping
.Pq the records are being discarded .
.It Sy applied_txg
The newest transaction group that the parity disks hold.
.It Sy pending_bytes
The space of the records that are not applied yet.
.It Sy held_bytes
The space of the freed blocks that ZFS holds until the records are applied.
.It Sy max_held_bytes
The limit of
.Sy held_bytes
in force
.Pq Sy parity_max_held .
.It Sy record_bytes
The space of the record objects that are not applied yet.
.It Sy max_record_bytes
The limit of
.Sy record_bytes
in force
.Pq Sy parity_max_records .
The write throttle starts when either use nears its limit.
.It Sy leaked_bytes
The space of held blocks that a drop could not free, because the record that
names them was unreadable.
The count stays after the records are gone.
ZFS cannot reuse this space;
.Nm zdb Fl b
reports it as leaked.
To get it back, use
.Nm zpool Cm parity Cm reclaim .
.El
.Pp
If the pool dropped its records at the hard limit, because of
.Sy parity_onstall Ns = Ns Sy continue ,
the output shows
.Qq not protected, resync needed .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl H
Scripted mode.
Do not display headers, and separate fields by a single tab instead of
arbitrary white space.
Each line has a field name, a tab and the value.
.It Fl p
Display numbers in parseable
.Pq exact
values.
.El
.It Xo
.Nm zpool
.Cm parity
.Cm drop
.Op Fl f
.Ar pool
.Xc
Discards all parity records of
.Ar pool
and releases all held freed blocks now.
The parity set must then resync the pool before the parity covers it again.
If
.Sy parityset
is
.Sy on ,
a new set of records starts.
If it is
.Sy off ,
the pool leaves the set.
.Pp
The command prints a warning and asks for a confirmation unless
.Fl f
is given.
A signal can interrupt the wait for the end of the drop.
The drop then goes on.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f
Do not ask for a confirmation.
.El
.It Xo
.Nm zpool
.Cm parity
.Cm reclaim
.Ar pool zdb-output
.Xc
or
.Xo
.Nm zpool
.Cm parity
.Cm reclaim
.Fl r
.Ar pool
.Xc
Frees the space that
.Sy leaked_bytes
counts.
The ranges come from
.Nm zdb ,
which finds space that is allocated and that no block pointer and no parity
record names.
Export the pool, then run
.Nm zdb Fl e Fl b Ar pool
and save its output.
The output has a reclaim header with the pool GUID, the uberblock txg, and
the error count.
Import the pool again and pass the output file to this command.
Use
.Fl -
to read the output from standard input.
The command uploads bounded batches and frees all ranges in one transaction.
.Pp
ZFS frees nothing unless all checks pass:
the header has no errors and names this pool, its txg is the state that the
import loaded, each range is allocated, no parity record holds a part of it,
and the sum of the ranges equals
.Sy leaked_bytes .
An empty list is an error.
.Pp
The
.Fl r
form explicitly resets the leaked-space count without freeing any ranges.
Use it only when you accept that the leaked space will stay allocated and
unavailable.
.El
.
.Sh STATISTICS
While a pool has parity state, ZFS publishes it in the kstat
.Pa /proc/spl/kstat/zfs/ Ns Ar pool Ns Pa /parity_set
on Linux, and in
.Sy kstat.zfs . Ns Ar pool Ns Sy .misc.parity_set
on
.Fx .
The values are read at the time of the read; no service is needed.
The kstat goes away when the pool is exported.
.Bl -tag -width "max_record_bytes"
.It Sy state
0 off, 1 on, 2 draining, 3 dropping.
.It Sy generation , set_guid
The parity record generation and the set that the pool belongs to.
.It Sy start_txg
The txg in which recording started.
.It Sy applied_txg , synced_txg
The last txg that the service applied, and the last txg that the pool synced.
.It Sy lag_txgs
.Sy synced_txg
minus
.Sy applied_txg
while recording is on, else 0.
.It Sy first_txg , last_txg , records
The oldest and newest txg with records that are not yet released, and their
number.
.It Sy pending_bytes , pending_txgs
Allocated bytes, and txgs, that the service has not applied yet.
.It Sy held_bytes , record_bytes
Freed space that is held until it is applied, and the space of the records.
.It Sy max_record_bytes , max_held_bytes
The limits in force for the write throttle.
.It Sy leaked_bytes
Held space that a drop could not free; see
.Nm zpool Cm parity reclaim .
.It Sy throttle
0 none, 1 soft (writers are delayed), 2 hard.
.It Sy throttled_ms
The time in milliseconds at the soft or hard level since the pool was loaded.
.It Sy applied_age_ms
The time in milliseconds since the service last applied a txg or reported
progress; 0 when it has not done so since the pool was loaded.
.It Sy unprotected , drop_pending
1 when records were lost and the set must resync the pool, and 1 while a drop
is pending.
.It Sy record_txgs , entries , released , drops
Counts since the pool was loaded: txgs that wrote records, record entries,
records released, and drops.
.It Sy repair_requests
Blocks that failed their checksum and asked the service for their bytes.
.It Sy repair_supplied , repair_declined , repair_rejected
Answers of the service to a read that waited: bytes taken, no bytes, and bytes
that failed the block checksum (also for
.Sy REPAIR ) .
.It Sy repair_written
Blocks that a
.Sy REPAIR
call wrote back.
.El
.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std zpool parity
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Example 1 : No Add a pool to a parity set
Set the property.
Records start a few transactions later.
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm set Sy parityset Ns = Ns Sy on Ar tank
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm parity status Ar tank
.
.Ss Example 2 : No Remove a pool from a parity set
Set the property to
.Sy off ,
wait until the parity software has applied the records, and check the state.
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm set Sy parityset Ns = Ns Sy off Ar tank
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm parity status Ar tank
If the parity software is not running, discard the records.
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm parity Cm drop Fl f Ar tank
.
.Ss Example 3 : No Reclaim the space that a drop leaked
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm export Ar tank
.Dl # Nm zdb Fl e Fl b Ar tank > Pa /tmp/leaks
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm import Ar tank
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm parity Cm reclaim Ar tank Pa /tmp/leaks
.
